@node autoeq Binary
@section The @code{autoeq} Binary
@cindex autoeq binary

The @code{autoeq} binary fits a small number of IIR filters to a
single measured curve (speaker on-axis / listening window, or a
headphone measurement). Usage:

@example
autoeq [OPTIONS]
@end example

@subheading Key flags

@multitable @columnfractions .35 .15 .50
@headitem Flag @tab Default @tab What it does
@item @code{-n, --num-filters} @tab 7 @tab Number of IIR filters.
@item @code{-c, --curve} @tab @tab Measured CSV (@code{freq,spl}); or
  use @code{--speaker --version --measurement} to fetch from the API.
@item @code{-t, --target} @tab @tab Target CSV (flat 0 dB if omitted).
@item @code{-s, --sample-rate} @tab 48000 @tab IIR design rate (Hz).
@item @code{--max-db / --min-db} @tab 3.0 / 1.0 @tab Per-filter gain
  bounds (absolute dB).
@item @code{--max-q / --min-q} @tab 3.0 / 1.0 @tab Per-filter Q bounds.
@item @code{--min-freq / --max-freq} @tab 60 / 16000 @tab Filter
  placement frequency bounds (Hz).
@item @code{--algo} @tab @code{nlopt:cobyla} @tab Optimiser back-end.
@item @code{--loss} @tab @code{speaker-flat} @tab Loss type
  (@pxref{autoeq User Guide}).
@item @code{--peq-model} @tab @code{pk} @tab PEQ filter structure.
@item @code{--maxeval} @tab 2000 @tab Optimiser evaluation budget.
@item @code{--refine} @tab off @tab Run local refinement after global.
@item @code{--local-algo} @tab @code{cobyla} @tab Local refiner.
@item @code{--smooth / --smooth-n} @tab on / 2 @tab 1/N-octave
  smoothing of the inverted target.
@item @code{--smoothness-weight} @tab 0.0 @tab TV^2 curvature
  regulariser (try 0.05 as a starting point).
@item @code{--min-spacing-oct} @tab 0.2 @tab Minimum inter-filter
  spacing in octaves.
@item @code{-o, --output} @tab @tab Output PNG for the result plot
  (requires @code{--features plotly}).
@item @code{--algo-list / --peq-model-list} @tab @tab List options
  and exit.
@end multitable

@subheading Examples

@strong{Headphone EQ from a CSV measurement, target Harman.}

@example
autoeq \
  --curve  measurements/my_headphone.csv \
  --target targets/harman_oe_2018.csv \
  --num-filters 10 \
  --min-freq 20 --max-freq 20000 \
  --max-db 6 --min-db 0.2 \
  --max-q 6 --min-q 0.5 \
  --algo autoeq:de --maxeval 5000 \
  --output result.png
@end example

@strong{Speaker EQ from the Spinorama API.}

@example
autoeq \
  --speaker     "Genelec 8341A" \
  --version     "vendor-pattern" \
  --measurement "CEA2034" \
  --curve-name  "Listening Window" \
  --num-filters 8 \
  --loss speaker-score \
  --algo autoeq:de --refine --local-algo cobyla \
  --output 8341a.png
@end example

@strong{Score-driven optimisation with smoothness regularisation.}

@example
autoeq \
  --curve   measurements/spk.csv \
  --target  targets/in_room_-0.8db.csv \
  --loss    speaker-score \
  --num-filters 6 \
  --smoothness-weight 0.05 \
  --smoothness-exponent 1.0 \
  --smoothness-schroeder-hz 250 \
  --algo autoeq:de --maxeval 4000
@end example

@strong{Multi-driver crossover optimisation} runs through a separate
code path triggered by passing a multi-driver speaker configuration;
in production this is exercised through @code{roomeq} (which can carry
multi-driver speakers in its config). The library entry point is
@code{autoeq::optim::optimize_multi_driver}.

@subheading Helper commands

@table @code
@item autoeq --algo-list
List every optimiser back-end with a short description.
@item autoeq --peq-model-list
List every supported PEQ model (peak only, peak + shelves, etc.).
@end table

